Zeldovich pancake
=================

A single sine-wave perturbation in an Einstein-de Sitter universe
collapses into a sheet (pancake). The initial conditions are set up at
z = 100 such that shell crossing (caustic formation) happens at z_c = 1.
The gas starts cold (T = 100 K). Before the caustic, the flow is a
smooth compression with an analytic solution; afterwards, the gas is
shock-heated in the sheet.

Running the test
----------------

  ./run.sh

generates the initial conditions (makeIC.py), runs SWIFT with hydro,
cosmology and self-gravity, and plots every snapshot with
plotSolution.py, which overplots the analytic solution (valid before the
caustic).

Validation scripts
------------------

The following scripts check two exact physical properties of the test.
They are useful to validate the treatment of the cosmological terms
(Hubble flow, scale-factor powers) and of the artificial dissipation of
a hydro scheme. They only need the run to proceed a little past the
caustic (z ~ 0.9); a_end in the parameter files can be reduced
accordingly.

1. checkAdiabaticity.py

   Before shell crossing there are no shocks, so the entropy
   A = (gamma - 1) u / rho^(gamma - 1) of every particle must be
   conserved. Any change is spurious heating (or cooling) by the
   artificial viscosity or conduction, for instance viscosity acting on
   pairs of particles that separate in the physical (peculiar + Hubble)
   frame.

   Usage (in the directory containing the snapshots):

     python3 checkAdiabaticity.py [basename] [z_check] [tolerance]

   Defaults: basename = zeldovichPancake, z_check = 3, tolerance = 1e-2.
   It prints the maximal and median relative entropy change of all the
   snapshots down to z_check, and returns a non-zero exit code if the
   maximal change exceeds the tolerance. Typical values for a correct
   scheme are a few 10^-3 at z = 3.

2. compareUnits.py (with zeldovichPancake_kpc.yml)

   The result of a simulation must not depend on the internal unit
   system. zeldovichPancake_kpc.yml describes the same physical problem
   as zeldovichPancake.yml, but with kpc instead of Mpc as the internal
   length unit. SWIFT converts the initial conditions (which carry their
   own units) when reading them. The softening lengths are rescaled;
   the time-step limits are left unchanged, as they are expressed as
   d log(a) in cosmological runs.

   Run both parameter files, e.g. in separate directories (two runs
   started in the same directory at the same time would compete for the
   restart directory), then collect the snapshots of both runs
   (zeldovichPancake_XXXX.hdf5 and zeldovichPancake_kpc_XXXX.hdf5) in one
   directory and run:

     python3 compareUnits.py [basename_1] [basename_2] [z_particle]

   Defaults: zeldovichPancake, zeldovichPancake_kpc, z_particle = 3.
   All quantities are converted to physical cgs units and the snapshots of
   the two runs are paired by redshift. For z >= z_particle the particles
   are compared one by one (tolerance 1e-3); later, binned profiles along x
   are compared instead (tolerance 5e-2 of the profile's range). The script
   returns a non-zero exit code if any comparison fails.

   A failure reveals a hidden dimensional constant in the scheme, e.g.
   an absolute epsilon added to a dimensional quantity or a missing
   factor of h. Particles are only compared one by one before z = 3: when
   the artificial viscosity first switches on in the centre of the pancake
   (z ~ 2.7), round-off differences are amplified to ~1e-2 in u. These do
   not come from the units: the same differences appear between two runs
   in the same units with different numbers of threads, which is a useful
   control run if the check fails.
